# c30 -- the keys a seek jumps over are still read.
#
# c11 has a cursor stop early, and the narrowing lets a write
# past the last key it examined through. A seek is the other
# shape: T1 reaches R8, so R8 is the last key examined, and
# everything below it is inside the predicate whether the
# cursor looked at it or not. T2 writes R5, which T1 skipped
# entirely, and the edge forms all the same. That is an
# over-approximation and it is the safe direction -- a
# predicate that tracked only the keys actually returned would
# miss this write, and with T2 --rw--> T1 the cycle would go
# undetected.


== pairing RR/RR == T1=RR T2=RR
@1 T1 begin -> ok
@1 T2 begin -> ok
@2 T1 open C R1 R9 -> ok
@3 T1 next C -> R1=1
@4 T1 seek C R8 -> ok
@5 T1 next C -> R8=8
@6 T1 close C -> ok
@7 T2 write R5 t2 -> ok
@8 T2 read R2 -> 2
@9 T1 write R2 t1 -> ok
@10 T1 commit? -> recorded
@10 T2 commit? -> recorded
@11 check committed T1 T2 -> satisfied
== pairing RR/S == T1=RR T2=SER
@1 T1 begin -> ok
@1 T2 begin -> ok
@2 T1 open C R1 R9 -> ok
@3 T1 next C -> R1=1
@4 T1 seek C R8 -> ok
@5 T1 next C -> R8=8
@6 T1 close C -> ok
@7 T2 write R5 t2 -> ok
@8 T2 read R2 -> 2
@9 T1 write R2 t1 -> ok
@10 T1 commit? -> recorded
@10 T2 commit? -> recorded
@11 check committed T1 T2 -> satisfied
== pairing S/RR == T1=SER T2=RR
@1 T1 begin -> ok
@1 T2 begin -> ok
@2 T1 open C R1 R9 -> ok
@3 T1 next C -> R1=1
@4 T1 seek C R8 -> ok
@5 T1 next C -> R8=8
@6 T1 close C -> ok
@7 T2 write R5 t2 -> ok
@8 T2 read R2 -> 2
@9 T1 write R2 t1 -> ok
@10 T1 commit? -> recorded
@10 T2 commit? -> recorded
@11 check committed T1 T2 -> satisfied
== pairing S/S == T1=SER T2=SER
@1 T1 begin -> ok
@1 T2 begin -> ok
@2 T1 open C R1 R9 -> ok
@3 T1 next C -> R1=1
@4 T1 seek C R8 -> ok
@5 T1 next C -> R8=8
@6 T1 close C -> ok
@7 T2 write R5 t2 -> ok
@8 T2 read R2 -> 2
@9 T1 write R2 t1 -> ok
@10 T1 commit? -> recorded
@10 T2 commit? -> recorded
@11 check at-most-one-committed T1 T2 -> satisfied
